<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">British Mauritius</h1><p class="st-meta">1810 CE – 1968 CE · capital Port Louis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The sugar island taken from France in 1810, transformed by Indian indentured labour into a mainly Indian-descended society.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> seized the French <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isle-de-france">Isle de France</a> in December 1810, landing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-malheureux">Cap Malheureux</a> months after a French naval victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-port">Grand Port</a>, in order to stop French raiders preying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> shipping. The island was ceded in the 1814 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a>, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, and its first governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-townsend-farquhar">Robert Farquhar</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The capitulation let Franco-Mauritian planters keep their French law, language and Catholic church, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> became the island's economy. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> ended in 1835, nearly half a million indentured labourers arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> through the immigration depot now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aapravasi-ghat">Aapravasi Ghat</a>, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims of Indian origin became the majority while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a> and French-speaking whites kept much economic power.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After constitutional reforms and the rise of the Labour Party, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> became independent on 12 March 1968 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seewoosagur-ramgoolam">Seewoosagur Ramgoolam</a>, and a republic in 1992. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagos-archipelago">Chagos Archipelago</a>, detached by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1965, remained disputed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-mauritius.jpg" alt="British Mauritius" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday Original uploader was Orange Tuesday at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mauritius_(1923%E2%80%931968).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1810 CE – 1968 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port Louis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert Farquhar, Seewoosagur Ramgoolam</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Christianity, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mauritian Creole, French, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritius">Mauritius</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Mauritius</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1889&amp;focus=british-mauritius" aria-label="British Mauritius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6230 2924 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Mauritius lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6550 3180L6550 3180M6550 3180L4997 651M6550 3180L6538 2607"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6550 3180h0M4997 651h0M6538 2607h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6550 3180h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6550 3180h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port Louis. <a href="/atlas?year=1889&amp;focus=british-mauritius">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
